  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold front is expected to stall along the coast\nas overall forcing weakens and shifts away from the region.\n With a more stable airmass in place behind the sea breeze and\ndry air aloft, thunderstorm chances are quickly diminishing for\nthe rest of the evening. However, some overnight redevelopment \nof showers and storms is possible as the front will remain in \nthe area, and elevated instability will remain abundant. Overall\nmoisture is not as deep as the past few days and the overall \nflooding threat is comparatively lower.\n\nGuidance continues to depict a weak surface low lifting NE along\nthe frontal boundary tomorrow (the low may actually subtly pull\nthe boundary farther inland as it progresses). Overall pre-storm\nenvironment will be similar tomorrow, sans a modest uptick in \neffective shear in the vicinity of the low. This may provide a \nmarginally better environment for supercell development. If the \nfront does get pulled west, the convective risk will also follow\nsuit. The greatest risk of strong to severe thunderstorms \nremains east of HWY 17 with damaging winds remaining the primary\nthreat, with hail possible in supercells.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance still struggling on whether or not the\nairmass can fully dry out Tuesday and Wednesday as our current\nfrontal boundary may still be stalled nearby. Isolated showers\nand storms cannot be ruled out but coverage is likely to be far\nsparser than the past few days. Moisture and instability then \nbegin to rebuild across the Southeast U.S. coastline with the \npotential for another round of unsettled weather.",
